                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Pretty solid sequel to the first game with notable improvements; the game is longer, puzzles have more variety, captivating art with the aesthetics feeling more creative and congruous instead of mostly relying on shambles as shock factor. The progression also allows more freedom due to movement on an actual map and multiple interaction points, instead of consisting of merely straight-forward levels (though searching where to continue can get needlessly time-consuming). \nI only despise how this game is worse optimized than the prequel - no resulution settings which causes the fullscreen to disrupt the hell out of my entire computer UI / tabs, no Steam overlay supported either. Though since I played this for the trashy low budget horror, so I shall deal with the technical execution being trashy too, I suppose? (Immersiveness bonus points in a way lmao). Lastly, the ending hints at another sequel but it never released to my knowledge. ☹",
